new file mode 100644
index 0000000..3e8ad44
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/adr/0002-address-posts-by-stable-slug.md
@@ -0,0 +1,58 @@
+---
+type: adr
+status: stable
+entities: [Post]
+related:
+  - app/Http/Controllers/PostController.ts
+  - app/Http/Resources/PostResource.ts
+  - app/Models/Post.ts
+  - app/Support/slug.ts
+  - routes/web.ts
+generated: { by: "human:Urata Daiki", at: 2026-08-18T07:35:53.308Z }
+---
+
+# Address posts by stable slug
+
+## Context
+
+Posts were addressed by their database id (`/posts/42`). Neither a reader
+nor a search engine learns anything from that number, and marketing asked
+for the title to appear in the URL before the next content push.
+
+Links that are already published point at the numeric addresses, so they
+cannot simply stop resolving.
+
+## Decision
+
+Every post carries a `slug` column (`posts.slug`, `NOT NULL UNIQUE`),
+derived from the title at creation time by `slugify()` in
+`app/Support/slug.ts`: lowercase ASCII letters and digits, words joined by
+a single `-`. `Post.generateSlug()` appends `-2`, `-3`, … until the slug is
+free, so two posts may share a title without sharing an address.
+
+A slug is assigned once and never rewritten. It is absent from
+`Post.fillable` and from `PostPayloadSchema`, so editing a title cannot
+touch it — the post keeps the address it was published at, and the slug the
+new title would have produced stays available for someone else.
+
+The post page moves to `GET /posts/:slug`. A router cannot distinguish that
+pattern from the old `/posts/:id`, so `PostController.show()` resolves an
+all-digits segment as an id first and answers with a **301** to the post's
+slug URL; if no post has that id, the value is tried as a slug (a title like
+"2026" slugifies to digits) before 404ing.
+
+Editing, updating and deleting stay on their id-based addresses — they are
+authenticated, never linked to from outside, and gain nothing from a
+readable URL.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- Post payloads sent to pages carry `slug`; the post list and the home
+  page's latest posts link to `/posts/<slug>`.
+- Posts written before this change were backfilled with `post-<id>` by the
+  `add_post_slug` migration: unique and well-formed, if not pretty.
+- Uniqueness is enforced by the column, but `generateSlug()` reads and
+  writes in separate statements. Two posts created with the same title at
+  the very same moment can therefore collide on the insert; the write fails
+  rather than producing a duplicate address. Retrying the insert, or moving
+  the numbering into the transaction, is the fix if that ever shows up.
